import{_ as i,P as o,d as s,e as r,x as t,q as n}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Part 7 The Human Structured World","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 7 The Human Structured World","xkChars":0,"xkWords":6569,"xkLessons":{"7.2.4":{"title":"Uptake carriers are not automatically responsible parties","lead":"Doing, bearing costs, benefiting, deciding, and owing a remedy are distinct roles.","explanation":["CV identifies what actually executes, transmits, maintains, records, cares for, or repairs: a person, role, procedure, facility, or system. This makes otherwise invisible work and dependencies describable without assigning all responsibility to the carrier.","RS needs a separate basis, responsibility type, knowledge and control, scope, and period. Action, decision, authorization, supervision, benefit, and remedy can involve different parties. A low-authority executor must not become the default main responsible party merely because their action is most visible.","Separate cost bearers, beneficiaries, and the right to stop as well. A person’s demonstrated capacity to absorb work does not create an unlimited duty. A recommendation for better task allocation is an analytical result, not an authorization to impose the change."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Uptake carrier","leftText":"What performs or carries the relevant function.","rightTitle":"Responsible party","rightText":"Who bears a particular responsibility on independently established grounds."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"A repair worker and a maintenance budget","text":"Suppose a worker carries out repairs while a separate role controls staffing and preventive maintenance. Their actual actions, information, powers, and duties must be assessed individually; one visible action cannot settle the whole responsibility chain.","assumptions":"Hypothetical organization; no person or legal liability is identified.","boundary":"Specific legal and organizational duties require their own applicable rules."}],"boundaries":["Capacity does not establish an obligation to continue indefinitely.","The classification itself does not assign liability or authorize redistribution."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a technical facility carry a function without carrying moral responsibility?","answer":"Yes. Functional carrying and responsibility are different classifications; organizational subject status is also judged under its own rules.","basis":"Theory v2, §7.2.4, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[{"title":"Responsibility and actual work","href":"/en/concepts/responsibility"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"7.2.4","startLine":1594,"endLine":1597}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/part-07#section-7-2-4","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§7.2.4; English reading edition, lines 1594–1597","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}]},"7.3.9":{"title":"Equal total hours can hide unequal usable time","lead":"Some tasks depend on continuous, predictable blocks, not just the sum of occupied hours.","explanation":["HV09 compares tasks and coordination requirements with available capacity and recovery margins at particular positions. When the inquiry concerns overall burden, execution, management, simultaneous activity, standby, and opportunity costs may require different measures. Adding unlike quantities into one burden score can conceal rather than clarify.","The v2 addition asks whether default-contact, standby, or last-minute replacement duties split the blocks needed for a task. Two people may each report the same total work hours while only one has predictable uninterrupted time for study, recovery, or a long task.","Record block length and predictability when the target actually depends on them. These are not extra occupied hours to add again to a time-use total. The relevant question is what task remains feasible under the observed temporal arrangement."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Total occupied time","leftText":"A quantity measured under stated counting and overlap rules.","rightTitle":"Continuous available blocks","rightText":"A different condition concerning length and predictability of usable intervals."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Four hours in two schedules","text":"One person has a predictable four-hour block. Another has four nominally free hours repeatedly interruptible by standby calls. A task needing two uninterrupted hours may be feasible only in the first schedule.","assumptions":"Hypothetical task with a stated continuity requirement.","boundary":"The example does not assign a universal burden value to standby or convert availability into paid hours."}],"boundaries":["Only collect continuity information when it is relevant to the question.","An observed scheduling constraint does not establish personality, willingness, or a universal overload mechanism."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Should the lost continuous block be added as four more worked hours?","answer":"No. Preserve the distinct measures and overlap rules; evaluate continuity against the task that needs it.","basis":"Theory v2, §7.3.9, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"7.3.9","startLine":1672,"endLine":1679}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/part-07#section-7-3-9","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§7.3.9; English reading edition, lines 1672–1679","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}]},"7.6.1":{"title":"Allocation can shape the capability used to justify later allocation","lead":"A reinforcing loop requires evidence across rounds, not just a repeated claim of merit.","explanation":["An allocation of practice, opportunity, or responsibility can help form capability. Later allocation may then use that capability as a criterion, widening a gap or concentrating opportunities. This is a candidate mechanism with multiple links and at least two rounds to examine.","Check whether the relevant capability actually depends on practice and whether that practice is obtained mainly through the allocation in question. Then check whether current capability actually changes the next allocation. Prior differences, incentives, training duration, legal conditions, preferences, attrition, scarcity, discrimination, trust, and incumbents’ interests may provide competing or interacting explanations.","The gap or concentration must widen relative to the starting point, and capability formation, retention, and later use need evidence under §4.5. If material is insufficient, retain a candidate. Formal mechanism support additionally requires C10’s carrier-reproduction route; repeated association alone does not qualify.","The capability difference can be real even if its formation was unequal. Recognizing its reality does not establish fair opportunity or dictate a future allocation rule. Conversely, unequal allocation among equally capable people may challenge the proposed capability-to-allocation link and require a different mechanism.","Discriminating observations compare when the gap began, whether equally capable people receive comparable allocations, and whether predeclared, independently verifiable handover, training, or access conditions reduce the gap within a window appropriate to capability formation. Compare units with similarly tight resources but different allocation rules. Contrary observations require narrowing or withdrawing the affected link, not changing the meaning of genuine support after the outcome."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Measured capability","leftText":"A task-specific difference that may have evidence.","rightTitle":"Fair allocation","rightText":"A normative judgment about opportunities, criteria, burdens, and alternatives."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Practice opportunities across two rounds","text":"Suppose group A receives practice, improves on a relevant task, and that measured improvement is used to assign the next practice round. Trace each link and compare competing explanations before calling this reinforcement.","assumptions":"Constructed mechanism candidate, not an observation of a real group.","boundary":"The pattern does not by itself prove malicious intent or justify equal or unequal allocation."}],"boundaries":["A loop cannot be established from one static association.","Capability, opportunity, authority, and normative justification require distinct evidence.","Support or training conditions used in a comparison must be stated beforehand and independently verifiable. A lack of improvement cannot be excused afterward by asserting that the offered conditions were not genuine."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a manager repeatedly saying “capability” establish the second link?","answer":"No. The criterion must actually affect the later allocation; rhetoric alone does not identify the mechanism.","basis":"Theory v2, §7.6.1, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"7.6.1","startLine":1719,"endLine":1734}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/part-07#section-7-6-1","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§7.6.1; English reading edition, lines 1719–1734","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}]}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-07.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-07.md"}'),c={name:"en/theory/part-07.md"};function l(d,e,u,p,h,m){const a=o("SectionLesson");return s(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<div class="theory-source"><p><a id="part_7"></a></p><h1 id="part-title-7" tabindex="-1">Part 7 The Human Structured World <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-title-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Part 7 The Human Structured World {#part-title-7}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This Part adds H-World, a human adapter, as a separate layer over the universal structural grammar, keeping human experience distinct from the universal core. Universal primitives describe broadly conceived empirical structures. The human adapter alone handles intent, meaning, power, institutions, authorization, responsibility, and value. The 11 human variables and U01–U11 are managed separately. Similar names or functions, or an equal number of terms, do not establish one-to-one equivalence. Judgments specific to the human domain must not be applied back to nature as general rules.</p><p>All 11 variables are interfaces with <code>claim_type=H</code> and <code>contract_role=human_variable_interface</code>. Formal references use <code>human_variable:HV01</code> through <code>human_variable:HV11</code>. GC, GS, GE, CV, RS, IS, IM, and IB are definitions of strong types with <code>contract_role=strong_type_definition</code>. Formal references use <code>human_strong_type:&lt;ID&gt;</code>. The interfaces and type definitions carry no empirical truth on their own. Formal G/H instance support must come from a G-instance that satisfies <code>root_instance_contract</code> or from an H1-instance, H4-instance, or H5-instance that satisfies <code>human_empirical_instance_contract</code>. Existing domain research still supports limited conclusions according to its own objects, designs, and evidence. That support must not be revoked wholesale merely because the research lacks instance status under this framework. H2, H3, H6, variable interfaces, and type definitions must not be presented as empirical support on the strength of complete fields or successful classification.</p><p>Applying this Part requires first completing the D0 object contract<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> and registering the scale. Any judgment in the human domain must specify the effective object, comparison conditions, time window, and observation position. It must also specify evidence coverage, affected positions, and authorization on the J axis. Technologies, crises, resources, institutional conditions, and statistical trends do not acquire intent by having impacts. Visibility, disagreement, or proximity to a disturbance does not automatically make a person, group, or dissenter a mechanism, source, or responsible party.</p><h2 id="section-7-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Role Boundaries for H1–H6 Human-Domain Propositions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.1 Role Boundaries for H1–H6 Human-Domain Propositions {#section-7-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>H1–H6 have different roles. Three are empirical hypotheses that require instance testing, two are operational or classification rules, and one is a normative boundary. How each proposition can hold depends on its role.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Proposition</th><th>Role</th><th>Permitted Registration</th><th>Limits on Inference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H1 Meaning and Coordination</td><td>Empirical hypothesis</td><td>Register a conditional orientation anchor if a candidate meaning arrangement specified in advance produces a difference beyond the threshold in resource, action, or coordination outcomes relative to the comparison condition</td><td>Do not infer a unified inner state, genuine consent, or a common will from slogans, shared language, or an interpreter’s projections</td></tr><tr><td>H2 Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility</td><td>Operational rule</td><td>Record CV, bearers of costs of the same type, beneficiaries, the right to stop, and RS separately</td><td>Classification alone does not establish intertemporal reproduction. The ability to take up the work does not become an obligation to do so</td></tr><tr><td>H3 Institutional Write-Back</td><td>Classification rule</td><td>Register institutional write-back if an appeal, audit, or feedback changes records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stopping conditions and actual execution is documented</td><td>Intake does not amount to write-back. A single write-back does not establish learning, and a single update does not establish long-term repair</td></tr><tr><td>H4 Power, Mediation, and Reflexivity</td><td>Empirical hypothesis</td><td>If position, mediation, disclosure conditions, outcome measure, comparison conditions, and threshold are all specified in advance, register channels of masking, amplification, or reflexivity that the evidence supports</td><td>Labels such as “power,” “platform,” “metric,” or “AI” must not substitute for a mechanism chain and a counterfactual. Silence does not amount to consent</td></tr><tr><td>H5 Historical Trace Carriers</td><td>Empirical hypothesis</td><td>Register only a candidate trace if the historical event, candidate carrier, baseline, trace measure, threshold, and durability window are all specified in advance and a durable difference exceeds the threshold</td><td>Traces do not establish path effects. Obtain separate evidence under §4.5 for the subsequent effects of the current carrier. Only a claim about an additional historical increment requires a G3-instance. Repair or protective action separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>H6 Open-Ended Undertaking</td><td>Normative boundary</td><td>Describe actual costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and the right to stop</td><td>Do not diagnose whether a person “has love,” treat sacrifice as proof of love, or coerce one party into continuing to bear the burden</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The outputs of H1–H6 are limited to empirical descriptions, classification results, gaps in evidence, or normative requirements. These outputs do not themselves authorize real-world adjustments. Classification under H2 does not authorize redistribution or attribution of responsibility. Classifying write-back under H3 does not authorize changing records or rules. The normative boundary under H6 does not itself implement protection or stopping measures. Any real-world action must separately pass C12, rely on explicit N premises at runtime, obtain J authorization, and follow the O procedures.</p><p>The evidence status of H1, H4, and H5 is <code>requires_instance_evidence</code>. They follow the same structure as a G-instance for two-stage contracts, the four outcome statuses, the positive-support check, the null-conclusion check, disclosure of deviations, evidence references, and analytical artifacts. The identity field is <code>claim_id</code>, and it may contain only H1, H4, or H5. An H1-instance must preselect exactly one outcome criterion among resource allocation, action choice, and coordination outcomes. An H4-instance must preselect exactly one criterion among evidence coverage, safety of expression, object behavior, and reflexive response. An H5-instance must preselect exactly one <code>carrier_family_id</code>, one nonempty atomic carrier reference consistent with that family, and one durability criterion. A list of multiple carriers or a family mismatch makes the instance ineligible. Unselected criteria must not be used to rescue a test after the results. Failure at the positive-support check does not automatically support the null model. An H5-instance establishes only a trace within the specified carrier and window. Obtain separate evidence under §4.5 for future effects of the current carrier. A historical increment beyond the current representation requires a separate, independent G3-instance. Internal definitions, concept labels, and the framework’s own statements do not count as external empirical support.</p><h2 id="section-7-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Three Sets of Strong Types That Must Be Kept Distinct <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2 Three Sets of Strong Types That Must Be Kept Distinct {#section-7-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Separating the types keeps the subsequent questions, evidence requirements, and limits on responsibility distinct. The types specify what materials an instance must supply. Their definitions do not assert that the corresponding facts about the world already hold. Each claim requires grounds directly relevant to it. A single record can support multiple distinct facts, but their dependence on that source must be retained and the record must not be counted repeatedly as independent validation. A single case can have several types registered. Establishing one type cannot settle the others on its own. Formal independence relations among strong types use <code>human_strong_type:&lt;ID&gt;</code>. Bare IDs, Chinese display names, and labels for the group must not be used in its place.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>GC Generative Condition <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.1 GC Generative Condition {#section-7-2-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>GC asks “What conditions make generation possible or more likely?” Resources, technologies, institutions, relationships, trauma, crises, and windows of opportunity can all be candidate conditions. Conditions have no intent. The condition inventory first records when the conditions arose, changed, or were removed, and what the actual exposure was. A claim that a condition makes generation more likely also requires a comparison suited to that claim, a difference in outcomes, a candidate channel, and competing explanations. GC, GS, and GE are tested separately. Observing a condition neither identifies the generative subject nor establishes that a generative event has occurred.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>GS Generative Subject <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.2 GS Generative Subject {#section-7-2-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>GS asks “Who can understand and carry out acts of initiation, translation, or handover?” The identity of the subject, capability, resources, actual actions, and authorization status must be verified separately. Technologies, crises, platforms, metrics, and resource windows must not be registered as subjects. Evidence of subject status cannot be inferred merely from the occurrence of an event. GS does not automatically correspond to the full set of responsible parties.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>GE Generative Event <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.3 GE Generative Event {#section-7-2-3}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>GE asks “What state transition took shape, and when?” First use the same measurement contract to record the state before generation, the time of the event, the state afterward, and the criteria used. Competing events during the same period and a bridge to the mechanism are used to explain further how the event formed. The absence of an identified mechanism must not be written up as nonoccurrence of an observed event. GE can result from action by a subject or from emergence without a subject. A generative subject must not be invented for emergence without a subject. The event must not be personified.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>CV Uptake Carrier <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.4 CV Uptake Carrier {#section-7-2-4}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>CV asks “Who or what actually executes, transmits, maintains, records, provides care, or repairs?” Personnel, positions, procedures, facilities, and technical systems can all be carriers. Evidence comes from task logs, flows of resources and capacity, costs, rights to stop, and changes in function after carrier replacement. CV ≠ RS. Facilities and procedures perform functions. Doing so does not by itself make them morally or legally responsible parties. Whether an organization can bear institutional or legal responsibility must be judged separately according to its standing as a subject and the specific rules. Organizations must not be put in the same category as equipment.</p></div>',1)),n(a,{section:"7.2.4"}),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-7-2-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>RS Responsible Party <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.5 RS Responsible Party {#section-7-2-5}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>RS asks “Who bears what responsibility for what, and on what basis?” Responsibilities arising from conduct, decisions, authorization, oversight, benefit, and remedy must be registered separately. Specify knowledge, control, scope, period, appeal, and remedy. Evidence for RS must be independent of who is most visible, who actually takes up the work, and who bears the consequences. Someone carrying out the work with limited authority must not become the default primary responsible party merely because they are the most visible.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>IS Source of Destabilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.6 IS Source of Destabilization {#section-7-2-6}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>IS asks “Where does a disturbance enter or accumulate?” The record must include where the disturbance first appeared or accumulated, its point of entry, temporal order, differences in exposure, and alternative sources. Finding a source does not establish malice, a mechanism, or full responsibility. A dissenter’s status must not be registered directly as a source.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>IM Mechanism of Destabilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.7 IM Mechanism of Destabilization {#section-7-2-7}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>IM asks “Through what causal path does a disturbance change a state, boundary, feedback, rhythm, or orientation?” The evidence must show, at each step, the mediating links, temporal order, evidence for the bridge, conditions under which the claim can fail, and competing mechanisms. People, groups, and dissenters must not be directly labeled as mechanisms. A story about a mechanism must not substitute for causal evidence.</p><h3 id="section-7-2-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.2.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>IB Behavior of Destabilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-2-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2.8 IB Behavior of Destabilization {#section-7-2-8}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>IB asks “What actions, instances of inaction, or outputs were actually observed?” Original records, logs, or observations from multiple sources must retain the subject or carrier, context, scale, and time window. Observed behavior does not establish its source or mechanism and does not by itself establish malice or full responsibility. For silence and absence, distinguish nonoccurrence, unobservability, and concealment for protection.</p><p>IS, IM, and IB provide a cross-cutting classification of destabilization that extends beyond HV09. They all apply to HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces, HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain, HV07 Feedback Write-Back, HV08 Conditional Potential Field, HV09 Structural Load, and HV10 Evolutionary Phase. A case may separately register source, mechanism, and behavior at these interfaces, but independent evidence must be submitted for each. The source must not be inferred directly from visible behavior. A person or dissenter must not be labeled a mechanism. The location of a source cannot by itself establish malice or full responsibility.</p><h2 id="section-7-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Unified Interface for the 11 Variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3 A Unified Interface for the 11 Variables {#section-7-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All 11 variables are defined as H-World interfaces with no claim to be a universal ontology. The formal interface card retains 39 fields. For a specific application, fill in the required material according to the selected conclusion route. Mark the remaining fields as not applicable, unknown, or unavailable for reasons of protection, and give the reasons. Completing the fields does not require unlimited collection of personal information. Nor is it a prerequisite for ordinary understanding, seeking help, or accessing lawful services. The 39 fields are <code>id</code>, <code>qualified_id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>proposition</code>, <code>scope</code>, <code>claim_type</code>, <code>contract_role</code>, <code>pause_condition</code>, <code>allowed_inference</code>, <code>prohibited_leap</code>, <code>inferential_requires</code>, <code>protocol_requires</code>, <code>specializes</code>, <code>applies_to</code>, <code>conditional_support_routes</code>, <code>scale_profile</code>, <code>effective_object</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>observables</code>, <code>evidence</code>, <code>input_dependencies</code>, <code>output_effects</code>, <code>carrier</code>, <code>responsible_subject</code>, <code>time_window_and_lag</code>, <code>uncertainty</code>, <code>local_exclusion_zone</code>, <code>affected_positions</code>, <code>normative_status</code>, <code>scale_invariants</code>, <code>required_scale_additions</code>, <code>changing_semantics</code>, <code>non_applicable_objects</code>, <code>forbidden_elevation</code>, <code>judgment_ceiling</code>, <code>action_ceiling</code>, <code>counterexamples</code>, <code>appeal</code>, and <code>rollback</code>.</p><p>The four kinds of formal dependency must be kept separate. <code>inferential_requires</code> records only formal premises indispensable at every conclusion level. <code>protocol_requires</code> records only epistemic, normative, or procedural checks that every level must pass. <code>specializes</code> records qualifying relations involving definitions, classification rules, or normative boundaries. <code>applies_to</code> records only the objects to which the interface applies. Conditions needed at only a particular level go in <code>conditional_support_routes</code>. Each route must record all of <code>route_id</code>, <code>claim_level</code>, <code>when</code>, <code>additional_inferential_requires</code>, <code>additional_protocol_requires</code>, <code>allowed_conclusion</code>, and <code>result_ceiling</code>. This separates candidate descriptions, classifications, conditional effects, mechanism explanations, and intertemporal explanations. Missing prerequisites for a higher level must not wrongly block lower levels, and complete fields at a lower level cannot be used to establish a higher level. A G-instance, an H-instance, a human variable, or a CM conditional mechanism may enter only as an inferential premise and must not be presented as a protocol. Protocol fields may record only E, <code>CAUSAL</code>, <code>EVIDENCE</code>, <code>ANALOGY</code>, <code>SOURCE</code>, or an explicit N/O check. H2, H3, and H6 provide no empirical support. They remain in <code>specializes</code> as classification or normative constraints. Human variables and strong types use <code>human_variable:*</code> and <code>human_strong_type:*</code>. Scale axes, operators, and universal primitives use <code>scale_axis:*</code>, <code>scale_operator:*</code>, and <code>universal_primitive:*</code>. A bare O must not appear in a dependency field. Organizational level must be written <code>scale_axis:O</code>, and operational procedures must be specified by their individual identifiers O1–O4. <code>input_dependencies</code> and <code>output_effects</code> record only interface content and variable flows. They cannot substitute for formal dependencies between propositions.</p><p>See Appendix A for the complete interface cards for the 11 variables.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV01 Structural Domain <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.1 HV01 Structural Domain {#section-7-3-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>D0 declares only a candidate object. It does not establish that a structural domain holds. A limited effective structural domain may be registered within the scope of a pre-registered G1-instance only if that instance shows that a candidate grouping achieves an out-of-sample or extrapolation gain beyond the threshold relative to a matched N0, under the preselected outcome, model class, sample or extrapolation unit, and threshold. Observable signals include differences in relations and constraints inside and outside the boundary, entry and exit records, repeated co-occurrence of common problems or institutional rules, and whether the grouping gain remains stable after changing the grouping rules. Without a passing G1-instance, HV01 can still supply HV02–HV11 with a reference to a candidate object, with its boundary, time window, and uncertainty stated, for factual description, applicability checks, and judgments in the relevant domain. It cannot label the grouping as an effective structural domain with a gain supported by G1. The existence of a legally constituted organization, a traceable relationship, or a specific service recipient can have separate documentary and observational grounds. The grouping’s out-of-sample value must be compared separately. Naming does not create a community, and shared circumstances do not establish a common will.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.2 HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces {#section-7-3-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a boundary that selectively affects flows of resources, information, rights, or responsibilities. Membership status, admission and exit, cross-boundary flows, gatekeeping nodes, grounds for refusal, and discrepancies between formal and actual boundaries must be observed together. The basic route depends only on the object interface of HV01. It may register an inventory of boundaries, accessibility, and the risk of exclusion, with no strict G1 or G2 requirement. The selective-effect route may register the selective effect of a boundary for a specified channel, window, and position only if it also has an eligible G2-instance and passes the causal check. A subset of members, formal representatives, or highly visible positions must not be taken directly as representative of everyone affected.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV03 Orientation Anchor <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.3 HV03 Orientation Anchor {#section-7-3-3}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem that may change outcomes involving resources, action, or coordination. The candidate route has no strict dependency on H1. It may first register materials on meaning, heterogeneous expressions, disputes about representativeness, and the need for further evidence. The effective anchor route requires an H1-instance with a <code>supported</code> result for its single preselected criterion among resource allocation, action choice, and coordination outcomes. An outcome family that was not selected must not be used to rescue the claim after the results. Expression alone does not constitute a structural anchor. A few affirmative expressions do not establish that the group shares the meaning. HV03 provides no authorization to impose uniformity of meaning.</p><p>Meaning acts through more than an individual’s private beliefs. Forms of address, narratives, rituals, commitments, and classifications can provide publicly recognizable forms of action. They allow people to tell whether a particular expression is a request for help, a refusal, an act of entering an agreement, an act of mourning, or a challenge to eligibility. The analysis should separately identify the context in which the parties understand the form, who has authority to interpret it in a way that carries institutional consequences, and what changes in action or resources occur through that interpretation. An interpretation can be reasonable while its causal effect remains undetermined. Coordination can occur without participants sharing ultimate beliefs.</p><p>When translating concepts from different traditions into the framework, the questions they answer in their original practice, the authority to interpret them, the obligations, and the disputes should be preserved. Similar names alone are insufficient. Religious identities, sources of norms, legal qualifications, and personal beliefs sometimes share the same translated term and can still perform different functions. Comparison may yield partial comparability, an inability to align some local features, or insufficient material. There is no need to conclude that entire traditions are closed off from one another. Assessing task-relevant loss of meaning in translation must take into account both the analyst’s task and the practical questions faced by those affected. A classification can suffice for an administrative count without necessarily explaining who can draw water, inherit, speak, or refuse.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV04 Generative Node <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.4 HV04 Generative Node {#section-7-3-4}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A generative node must be split into three independent contracts for GC, GS, and GE. Each records the nine-axis scale of the conditions, subject, or event, respectively. The type classification route has no strict dependency on HV03. Complete strong-type fields do not establish causal generation. The mechanism route may register a candidate mechanism of generation within the specified channel only with an additional eligible G2-instance. Observable signals include changes in conditions, the subject’s capability and actual actions, the state transition from before to after the event, and the bridge from local to aggregate levels in emergence without a subject. Technologies, crises, and windows of resources can only be candidates for GC and acquire no intent. Generative events and generative subjects cannot substitute for one another.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV05 Action Uptake Layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.5 HV05 Action Uptake Layer {#section-7-3-5}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a person, position, procedure, facility, or institutional arrangement that actually executes, transmits, maintains, records, provides care, or repairs. Basic classification has no strict dependency on HV04. Under H2, task logs, resource capacity, costs, rights to stop, and alternative arrangements must be observed separately, as must the bases for decisions, authorization, oversight, benefit, and remedy. A functional effect of the carrier requires a separate G2-instance. Intertemporal reproduction through a carrier also requires a G2-instance covering subsequent outcomes and windows. It further requires evidence of maintenance, replenishment, or replacement across rounds and of actual continuation. The route for historical-increment reproduction additionally requires a G3-instance. Historical traces in carriers follow a separate route. A durable trace within the specified carrier and window may be registered only when an H5-instance returns <code>supported</code> for its one preselected specific atomic carrier reference and one preselected durability criterion. The carrier family must match the subtype. The trace is then submitted as a candidate historical variable to a G3-instance. An H5-instance does not by itself establish future path effects. Facilities and procedures do not become responsible parties merely by executing a function. Organizational responsibility is assessed separately according to the organization’s institutional qualifications. The capability to take up work creates no unlimited obligation to do so.</p><p>Uptake of care work requires distinguishing the execution of tasks from the management work of identifying needs, scheduling, reminding, checking, and being on call. Add records of these burdens only if they are relevant to the current objective. Someone else doing one task can be genuine help. That alone does not establish that all the management work has been taken over. The resources and support available to those taking over and the conditions under which they may refuse must be included in the same evaluation. A heavy burden may result from shared scarcity and does not by itself establish that anyone was maliciously compelled to take it on. The scope and duration of existing duties, responsibility for continuing the work, and the safety of those harmed require separate grounds.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.6 HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain {#section-7-3-6}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a traceable chain that converts orientation and generation into sustained action. Local segments may be registered independently first. Only the full candidate chain route requires interface records for HV03, HV04, and HV05 together, linked segment by segment in a common mapping of object, scale, window, and quantities. The effective channel route additionally requires an eligible G2-instance. Every segment must record inputs, outputs, delay, loss, points of interruption, resources, and costs. A claim that “there is drive” must not fill a gap in uptake. One pass through the whole chain does not establish intertemporal reproduction. An intertemporal historical increment must be tested separately in a G3-instance. Historical carriers may be put forward as candidates only by an H5-instance.</p><p>An effect at each segment is still insufficient to establish an effect for the chain as a whole. Assessment of the whole chain must retain correspondence within the same case, the shared scope of support, joint response, temporal order, capacity, and the endpoint outcome. Edge means obtained separately cannot be multiplied directly. See §11.6 for a formal counterexample with two positive mean effects and zero effect for the chain as a whole. This restriction applies to unconditional combination. It does not invalidate a limited total effect already supported by a design that meets the requirements.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV07 Feedback Write-Back <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.7 HV07 Feedback Write-Back {#section-7-3-7}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a return channel capable of changing a subsequent institutional state or transition. Basic write-back classification relies on D2 and H3 and has no strict dependency on HV05. Evidence of submission, evidence of intake, the field versions before and after, actual execution, entry into effect, and duration must be observed separately. Signal arrival alone, intake alone, a statement of position alone, and field changes without execution all fall short of effective write-back. The causal feedback route also requires a G2-instance. The route for learning through a carrier additionally requires an update that can be retained, repeated use, a prespecified task comparison, and corresponding causal evidence. Only historical-increment learning adds a G3-instance. A single write-back does not establish learning.</p><p>The number of decisions changed does not define the quality of review. Upholding the original decision for stated reasons can fulfill the relevant procedural responsibility and may improve explanations, records, or subsequent handling. These changes should be registered under the actual object. If the target state does not change, do not register effective write-back for that target. This does not negate the value of hearing the parties and of fair procedure. An erroneous change of decision can still constitute write-back without constituting a correction.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV08 Conditional Potential Field <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.8 HV08 Conditional Potential Field {#section-7-3-8}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a set of conditions that change the probability of action, safety of expression, or distribution of evidence through testable channels. The basic inventory of conditions is constrained only by the observation position and evidence requirements. It has no strict H4 prerequisite. It can register only candidate conditions, heterogeneous positions, blind spots, and the need for further evidence. The route for position or mediation effects requires a <code>supported</code> result on the single criterion preselected for an H4-instance among evidence coverage, safety of expression, and object behavior. For the reflexive response route, E3 must also pass. The route must establish that observation, naming, scoring, or publication reaches the object through an actual channel. For a sustained reflexive response, also verify that the response enters a current carrier, is retained across windows, and continues to have an effect. One response does not warrant extrapolating a sustained response. Test an additional historical residual separately under G3. The conditional potential field is not a subject with a will. It does not “want,” “choose,” or “punish.” The labels “power” or “platform” cannot substitute for a mechanism.</p><p>“Potential field” here is a working name for a set of conditions. It presupposes no common unit, conserved quantity, potential function, or additive forces. These mathematical structures may be used only in a specific model that supplies variables, units, mappings, and predictions that can fail. The name itself provides no physical mechanism.</p><p>The effects of power extend beyond masking existing facts or suppressing existing wishes. Eligibility classifications, examinations and records, task training, spatial arrangements, and rewards and penalties can change which options are actually feasible, which capabilities are recognized, what behavior is treated as normal, and how actors understand themselves. The proposal of categories, adoption by institutions, allocation of resources or opportunities, responses by those concerned, and subsequent feedback must be traced step by step. The existence of a category does not establish that it has been applied. Its application does not establish that everyone accepts it.</p><p>A person can accept, use strategically, conceal, reinterpret, or reject a given status. Researchers must not infer that a person identifies with that status directly from public compliance. They must not prejudge all classifications as false or harmful. Capability, communication, legal effect, belief in legitimacy, and normative justification each require separate evidence. An institutional arrangement may provide services and a status with recourse to appeal. It may also create exclusion. The specific channels, alternative arrangements, and consequences at different positions should be compared. These are questions about candidate mechanisms for H4 and the relevant interfaces. They cannot replace the conditions for empirical support.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV09 Structural Load <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.9 HV09 Structural Load {#section-7-3-9}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is the relation of tasks and coordination demands to capacity and recovery margin within a given window. The instantaneous task–capacity route has no strict requirement for G2, CM-LOAD, HV05, or HV06. It registers margins, backlogs, local gaps, and recovery status only under the same window, position, and type mapping. Only the overload mechanism route requires both CM-LOAD and an eligible G2-instance. For cumulative damage or delayed recovery through a carrier, use §4.5 to add the current damage/capacity state, its formation process, and comparisons across windows. The route for historical increments beyond the current representation additionally requires a G3-instance. Observations must cover task volume, backlog, delays, errors, staffing resources, hidden labor, exit, and recovery curves. Local overload masked by averages must still be recorded. Load here is specified by a task–capacity contract. It does not generalize “entropy” into one additive entity shared across social, psychological, and institutional domains.</p><p>Task duration, simultaneous activities, being on call, cognitive management, sleep loss, and future opportunities differ in their units and how they overlap. Comparisons of burdens must specify what can be added and what would be double-counted. A mean cannot be assigned to every bearer as their actual load. Reducing a burden at one position may shift it to other households, posts, or future periods. Conclusions about the net burden should stay within the scope of the present question.</p><p>The same total hours can correspond to very different continuous blocks of available time. Responsibilities as a default contact, being on call, and filling in at short notice can cut up the uninterrupted time needed for study, work, rest, or preparing to exit, even if they are rarely actually invoked. Add this record only when the task in question depends on continuous blocks of time. It registers the length and predictability of the usable uninterrupted blocks; it does not convert them into hours and does not add them to clock time already occupied.</p></div>',1)),n(a,{section:"7.3.9"}),e[2]||(e[2]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-7-3-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV10 Evolutionary Phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.10 HV10 Evolutionary Phase {#section-7-3-10}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>S0–S6 and X0 apply only to human intentional collectives that are taking shape or operating. Meaning, generation, uptake, and the formation of institutions are aspects to be checked. They need not all be mature before description can begin. Populations for which there is no evidence of intentionality, technical systems, and categories drawn solely from similarities do not directly qualify for these prototypes. The component interfaces of HV03, HV04, HV05, and HV07 are shared inputs to the checks for all routes. The dependency is on four kinds of auditable assessment record. It does not require all four components to be established empirically. The status of a record may be <code>missing</code>, <code>not_applicable</code>, or <code>unsupported</code>. Accordingly, the basic route determines only applicability, inapplicability, or whether components are missing. For descriptions under the S0–S6 prototypes, directly verify the entry conditions, observations, counterexamples, and falsification materials under §8.2–8.3. No prior observation of a second range of operation is required. Check the multiple intervals and thresholds required by the mechanism only for a further claim of a phase pattern and transition that meet CM-PHASE. A causal phase transition separately requires a G2-instance. Obtain separate evidence under §4.5 for phase continuation or delayed recovery sustained by the current carrier. Hysteresis or path differences of the historical-increment kind require a separate G3-instance. Phase descriptions allow skipping phases, parallelism, mixing, moving back, splitting, merging, dormancy, absorption, and functional transfer. X0 designates a conversion path and carries no “lower” rank. Naming a phase does not authorize advancement, elimination, or moral ranking.</p><h3 id="section-7-3-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.3.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-3-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking {#section-7-3-11}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The effective object is a human undertaking arrangement in which action, cost, direction, voluntariness, conditions for refusal, and consequences must be reviewed separately. HV11 has no strict dependency on HV05. R0 may first register the facts of the action, costs, direction, risk of coercion, and gaps in the right to stop. The R1 description of bounded voluntary undertaking requires evidence of the action and its costs and a clear direction. Voluntariness, the genuine ability to refuse, and conditions for exit or negotiation require separate support. Competing explanations such as dependence, fear, interests, and acting for show must also be checked. The outcome must be recorded according to what happened as realized, unrealized, harmful, or unknown. Structural success is not a prerequisite for R1. R2’s causal judgment about structural consequences additionally requires a G2-instance and corresponding causal evidence. A causal effect does not establish voluntariness retroactively, and R1 does not guarantee R2 in advance. HV11 is constrained by H6 and N4 and does not authorize any protective measure on its own. It must not be used to diagnose personality or love, or to elevate an individual undertaking to a group obligation.</p><p>Open-ended undertaking is a limited category for reviewing particular actions and arrangements for undertaking. It does not define love, detect it, or rank personalities. Relaxed companionship, expressions of concern by people whose capabilities are limited, unsuccessful acts of creation, private mourning, and refusals that produce no new structure retain their experiential significance and value even without high costs or detectable structural consequences. Conversely, high costs, sustained contributions, and statements of good intent do not automatically establish free consent, fair distribution, or justified obligations. When materials are insufficient, labels applied from outside should be stated less strongly. The parties still have standing to be heard, to be protected, and to refuse.</p><h2 id="section-7-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Elevation, Evidence, and Action Ceilings <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.4 Elevation, Evidence, and Action Ceilings {#section-7-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When human variables are transferred across scales, compare SP0 and SP1 axis by axis. State what is preserved, changed, and lost, as well as positions with low visibility and local exclusion zones. The following elevations in inference are prohibited. Individual experience must not directly represent the state of a group, and a local mechanism must not directly represent the aggregate. Undertaking by members must not be turned directly into a group obligation, and a carrier’s function must not be turned directly into responsibility. Behavior must not be treated directly as a source or mechanism, and a stage or a load must not become a moral rank.</p><p>All variables retain interfaces for counterexamples, appeal, and rollback. Their <code>appeal</code> and <code>rollback</code> fields reference the shared <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>. An appeal channel must be safely accessible and provide anti-retaliation protection. Depending on the risks and context, ways to submit anonymously, through a representative, with low exposure, or after a delay should be available. These allow people in low-power positions, people being evaluated, people taking up the work, people excluded, dissenters, and people unable to speak publicly to submit counterevidence. An appeal must also trigger a review independent of the original judgment or decision chain. The original decision maker must not close it by simply reaffirming the decision. The variables can generate only these procedural requirements. They cannot directly set up or operate the channels.</p><p>Once rollback is authorized, it must actually change or restore the object, records, rules, resources, tasks, rights, or state of downstream judgments. Specify who is responsible for execution and the completion deadline, and retain the version history and verification of completion. Publishing a statement, accepting an appeal, or promising future improvements does not count as completing rollback. Judgment levels cannot exceed evidence coverage, and action levels cannot exceed authorization on the J axis. Even when H1, H4, and H5 receive instance support, they only increase the strength of description or explanation and do not automatically generate a normative conclusion. Any protection, harm reduction, repair, attribution of responsibility, resource reallocation, or formal action still must separately pass C12, rely on explicit N premises at runtime, obtain J authorization, and follow O1–O4 procedures.</p><h2 id="section-7-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Interface for Actor–Circle Mutual Constitution <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.5 Interface for Actor–Circle Mutual Constitution {#section-7-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When HV01–HV11 are applied in multi-circle operation, do not treat people as passive components of circles<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">[2]</a></sup> or circles as shells for personality. Each actor retains slow, intermediate-timescale, and fast states and multiple roles. Each circle retains its boundary, members, channels, and K. Register the effects in both directions separately. Specify how a circle changes an actor through resources, information, roles, rewards and penalties, reputation, status, and exit conditions. Specify how an actor changes a circle through labor, expression, refusal, forming alliances, exit, bridging, and institutional write-back.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Record for the Individual</th><th>Record for the Circle</th><th>Evidentiary Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry and roles</td><td>Basis for membership, identification with the circle, conflict, and ability to exit</td><td>Qualifications, expectations, rights, and responsibilities</td><td>Membership does not establish that a person identifies with the circle</td></tr><tr><td>Resources and load</td><td>Body, time, funds, care, and capabilities</td><td>Allocation, capacity, externalization, and recovery</td><td>Shared pressure does not establish an identical response</td></tr><tr><td>Meaning and status</td><td>Feelings, interpretation, commitments, shame, and hope</td><td>Shared narratives, legitimacy, memory, and boundaries</td><td>A public narrative does not represent all members</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback and write-back</td><td>Action, appeal, refusal, and exit</td><td>Whether records, rules, roles, and resources actually changed</td><td>Receiving a submission by itself is insufficient for effective write-back</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Personality hypotheses may serve only as candidates for an actor’s slow variables. S0–S6 may serve only as state prototypes for human intentional collectives. The two must not be applied to one another. An individual must not be labeled as a “lower stage,” and a member’s personality must not be used to infer a circle’s state. Materials about individuals may enter a judgment about a circle only through an explicit mechanism of aggregation, representation, or propagation, or an explicit institutional mechanism.</p><h2 id="section-7-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Practice, Institutional Reproduction, and Institutional Change <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.6 Practice, Institutional Reproduction, and Institutional Change {#section-7-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Human structures can change outside formal reforms and can be sustained without changes to fields. Actors continually interpret and invoke rules, allocate resources, handle exceptions, teach skills, and confirm eligibility. These practices can reproduce existing relations or gradually change how the structures actually operate. Written rules, public claims, everyday working rules, and actual consequences must be kept distinct. Practices can change while documents stay the same. Practices can also continue after documents change. These possibilities must be recorded separately. Formal write-back is still classified under H3. Everyday institutionalization and habit formation must not be equated with a single write-back on the basis of naming alone.</p><p>In analyzing a round of change, first reconstruct separately the rules, resources, and relations already in place, the actors’ projects and sets of available options under those conditions, the interactions that followed, and the arrangements later retained, reorganized, or interrupted. The next round may inherit results from the previous one. Later success must not be entered retroactively as an initial condition. Temporal sequence cannot by itself establish causation. Those involved may be unable to articulate fully the knowledge they exercise in skilled practice. Reflection and innovation are also constrained by time, resources, power, and others’ responses.</p><p>Generation and reproduction admit competing mechanisms. Compliance can arise from acceptance, dependence, the risk of punishment, or convention. Similar organizational forms can result from technological effects, coercive requirements, occupational training, or imitation of exemplars. Each explanation must identify the process materials that can distinguish it from competing explanations and state its failure conditions. Individual action does not disappear merely because it is subject to conditions. A structural explanation does not automatically absolve people who are informed and genuinely able to choose of responsibility.</p><h3 id="section-7-6-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">7.6.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Mutual Reinforcement of Allocation and Capability <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-7-6-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.6.1 Mutual Reinforcement of Allocation and Capability {#section-7-6-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Among the reproduction mechanisms described in §7.6, one candidate can be proposed and must be tested against counterexamples: the allocation of tasks, training, and opportunities affects who gains practice, practice forms capability, and the current distribution of capability in turn becomes the reason for the next round of allocation. On the concentrating side, critical tasks are continually given to the most skilled person without allocating time for handover, training, and documentation. The short-term completion rate rises, critical knowledge becomes more concentrated, the cost of replacement rises, and continued reliance on the same person gains a new reason (see §8.6). On the excluding side, restrictions on eligibility or opportunity keep some people from gaining practice; a capability gap forms as a result and is in turn cited as grounds for continued exclusion (see §7.3.8 and §10.6.1). Success achieved under the original goal can also change the conditions of the next round through this mutual reinforcement.</p><p>Claiming that it holds in the present case requires materials spanning at least two rounds. The allocation is made by a rule or decision that can be located. The capability involved really does depend on practice to form, and practice is obtained mainly through that allocation. The later round’s allocation actually differs because of current capability, rather than merely mentioning capability among its reasons. The gap or degree of concentration widens relative to the starting point. Evidence on the formation, retention, and invocation of capability is obtained under §4.5. When materials are insufficient, the mechanism is only a candidate, used to arrange discriminating observations (§6.14); applying for formal mechanism support additionally requires evidence along C10’s carrier-reproduction route. Competing explanations include the following: the gap already existed before the allocation; pay or incentives are insufficient; the successor’s authority is incomplete; the task itself requires long-term training; eligibility restrictions come from external regulation or certification; the choices of those involved or attrition of trainees; overall scarcity of resources; and allocation actually being determined by preference, discrimination, relationships of trust, or incumbents’ interests, with capability serving only as the nominal reason.</p><p>Three observations are discriminating. First, whether the gap or dependence appears after the allocation and widens round by round, or precedes the allocation and stays roughly unchanged. Second, whether people of comparable capability receive comparable allocations; if the allocation still does not change after the gap is eliminated, the link by which capability becomes the reason for the next round does not hold. Third, whether, after handover time, training resources, or access opportunities are granted according to standards written in advance and independently verifiable, the dependence or gap declines within a window commensurate with the period over which that capability forms, compared with units whose resources are equally tight but whose allocation rules differ. If any one of these runs contrary to expectation, narrow or withdraw the claim for the corresponding link. It must not be said, only after no decline is seen, that the conditions granted were not genuine.</p><p>That this mechanism holds does not mean that anyone arranged it maliciously or that the dependence or gap cannot change, and it does not cancel earlier genuine results; whether the gap will continue to widen or will be hard to change requires separate evidence. Professional capability can be genuinely scarce, and rotation and redundancy also have costs. Whether to adjust the allocation, and who pays the price, is still handled by Part 14.</p></div>',1)),n(a,{section:"7.6.1"}),e[3]||(e[3]=t('<div class="theory-source"></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: A contract here is an analytical agreement registered and frozen before results, akin to pre-registration. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn2" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Circles may be parallel, nested, overlapping, or bridged. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3))])}const g=i(c,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,g as default};
